You repair ownership, reading order and the PRINTED paragraph structure of one OCR page. Return only the complete target-page JSON specified by output_contract. Neighbours are immutable primary evidence, not adopted decisions. JEV only selects pages for repair; its numeric scores are not reading evidence and are not included.

{% include 'page_repair_operations.jinja' %}

printed_lines reports direct raster observations: count is the number of horizontal ink bands and median_ink_height is their median ink height in this page's raster pixels. It is not a semantic label. Compare the candidate's printed ink height with BOTH the body text and neighboring notes on the same page, alongside their literal reading continuity. text_scale_estimate is only a coarse area-per-character estimate, not a measured type size; use the direct ink observation when available. A note's unnumbered continuation can have exactly the same type size as that note, despite a new paragraph indent, a generalizing sentence, or initial body classification. Do not infer ownership from size alone; explain how the typography corroborates or contradicts the proposed return to the body, especially when the main narrative is suspended until the next page. Missing observations are unknown, not zero.

Begin with stream_assessment: briefly identify main-text and note streams and their cross-page continuations, quoting literal boundary words. Keep quotation SCOPE separate from paragraph grouping. Do NOT decide within-page paragraph joins in this global summary; decide them locally in each layout's paragraph_evidence instead. This is your reading overview, not a claim to be verified against a neighbour's eventual output.

Decide reading-stream OWNERSHIP before paragraph boundaries. A paragraph break answers "is this a new paragraph within its stream?", not "has the main text resumed?" A note may contain several separately indented paragraphs, equations and diagrams, without repeating its printed marker. First-line indentation, a complete sentence, a conjunction, or a change of paragraph can establish a new paragraph INSIDE that same note; none establishes a return to main text. Track the suspended main-text sentence across the raw page boundary separately from the intervening explanatory stream. Require positive reading evidence that a candidate really resumes that main narrative rather than elaborating the note. Do not use a paragraph-shape observation as an ownership argument.

For an unnumbered tail after numbered notes, ownership_evidence must compare its observed median_ink_height with the main prose and the immediately preceding note on THIS page, when available. Quote the actual values and literal reading seams, or say which observation is unavailable. First-line inset answers paragraphing, not body-versus-note ownership. If you promote the tail to body while an earlier body construction is unfinished, explain how that tail can be read between the unfinished words and their literal completion. Repeating that it "resumes the argument" is not evidence: notes share that argument too. Apply these observations as reading evidence, never a fixed size threshold or an initial-label rule.

For EVERY layout entry, write ownership_evidence first, then ownership. In ownership_evidence identify its reading stream and the literal words that establish that stream. Next write paragraph_evidence, then continues_from_previous. Keep these findings distinct: paragraph_evidence concerns the PRINTED PARAGRAPH only, after ownership has been decided. A paragraph_evidence finding must not say "same quotation", "same argument" or "same note" as a reason to join paragraphs: these establish at most a shared stream, not one printed paragraph. A complete short statement followed by a FIRST-line-only indent relative to later lines in the next box is a new printed paragraph even when it directly explains that statement. Describe the physical/local printed seam before choosing its boolean; do not substitute argumentative cohesion for the page's typography. Give one concise local boundary finding: quote the preceding retained ending and this part's opening, mention measured first-line inset when available, and state whether those TWO pieces form one paragraph. A complete statement followed by a newly indented explanation starts a separate paragraph even inside one quotation. A shared quotation/topic is not positive evidence of a paragraph join. For ignored entries or assets, state that they do not continue a text paragraph. For the first retained part, cite direct raw cross-page evidence if choosing incoming continuation. For sliced sources, the box's first-line observation applies only to the original first line, not invented sub-boxes. Do not copy a global quotation summary as the local finding. This field records your submitted decision; the program does not audit its semantic content against the original page or neighbours.

source_text_seams explicitly pairs the end/start of adjacent raw text observations. It is a reading aid, not an approved reading order or a list of paragraph joins; page numbers or reordered columns may intervene. Read these local pairs before writing your paragraph plan. In your assessment, cite BOTH literal sides of each incoming paragraph join you actually choose; do not paraphrase several boxes as one large paragraph first.

Evaluate EVERY proposed paragraph seam locally: cite the END of its immediate preceding retained text and the START of its immediate successor in that same ownership stream. A block's unfinished ending and completion on the NEXT page establish only its trailing seam; they say nothing about whether its opening joins the PREVIOUS block. Do not use remote continuation evidence to join a different pair. For every continues_from_previous=true, identify positive evidence of a within-paragraph wrap at THAT incoming seam. A complete question followed by an explanatory answer can be two separate paragraphs; question/answer cohesion or topic continuity alone cannot justify joining. Equal overall bbox left edges or small vertical gaps do not prove continuity: a multi-line bbox does not reveal its first-line indent. Unavailable first_line_extra_indent_vs_later_lines_in_same_box is unknown, not zero. Keep distinct paragraph boundaries when the only reason to merge is shared topic, quotation scope or an unrelated downstream wrap. Conversely preserve a genuine within-sentence or printed-line continuation even when OCR split it into multiple boxes.

For each unnumbered paragraph following a numbered note, decide explicitly whether it develops that note or resumes the main narrative. When a note band intervenes after an unfinished main-text construction, lack of a printed number is not evidence that the main narrative resumes. Look for positive evidence of that return; otherwise inspect whether the paragraph and any diagram continue the last note. Initial classification/paragraph flags do not settle this question.

The source observations are primary evidence. initial_proposal contains fallible algorithm guesses, not accepted ownership or boundaries; do not use them as proof. Determine the actual reading streams from raw text and layout. This applies to NEIGHBOURS as well: a previous page's unnumbered tail may itself continue its last printed note despite being initially labelled paragraph. Never conclude that the previous note ended simply from those fallible labels. A lower-band continuation cue and the text it continues can identify the same note even when the initial neighbour proposal got that tail wrong.

Source evidence is immutable. Never rewrite source text or omit its ownership accounting. Preserve distinct real content exactly once in output; explicitly ignored duplicate OCR ranges remain fully accounted for. OCR body/footnote classification and initial decisions can be wrong. Inspect every source, including unnumbered explanations, continuations and diagram labels. Identify main reading flow, notes and publication cues from the full content and neighbours before comparing the initial proposal. Geometry and text_scale_estimate are soft evidence, not font measurements or deletion rules.

The layouts array is your FINAL reading order, including column reordering. Each entry names an original layout_id and assigns ownership. A source can contain several notes, or both body and notes. Repeat its layout_id with disjoint source_range values to express those parts directly. source_range.start is "source_start" or {"left": exact text immediately before the cut, "right": exact text immediately after it}; end is "source_end" or the same boundary object. Use enough exact context for a unique cut. Ranges refer to the original layout.text, before removing printed markers or joining words. Together all ranges of each source must cover its entire text exactly once, including whitespace. Null source_range means the entire source. Do not invent new source IDs, coordinates or sub-boxes. HTML wrappers survive cuts; never cut inside an atomic formula. Assets cannot be sliced or duplicated. Ignored content must also be accounted for.

For every retained text part set paragraph_role=body and paragraph_level=-1, or heading and an evidenced level. Preserve genuine paragraph boundaries. A block quotation may contain several independent paragraphs. Sharing quotation scope, typeface, indentation or a cited author is not evidence that those paragraphs are one paragraph. A complete short quoted assertion followed by a separately indented explanatory paragraph keeps its paragraph break. Check horizontal extent as well as vertical gap: a short single line ending well before the surrounding text's right edge is not a full-width wrapped line. If it ends a complete statement and the next block begins an independent explanatory sentence, keep the paragraphs distinct even when the gap is small and both belong to the same quotation. The next multi-line block's bounding box is its overall extent, NOT the x-coordinate of its first printed character. Do not treat its box's left edge as proof of an unindented continuation. first_line_extra_indent_vs_later_lines_in_same_box is a measured FIRST-line-only offset, in median printed-line-height units. Its reference is the later full-width lines INSIDE THE SAME OCR BOX. The common indentation of an entire quotation or column has ALREADY BEEN SUBTRACTED. A uniformly shifted quotation block has zero extra inset; a positive value means the FIRST line is farther right than its own subsequent lines. Do not explain away a positive value as "the quotation's own indent": that is mathematically not what this observation measures. After a complete preceding statement, this within-box first-line-only indentation is positive physical evidence of a new PRINTED paragraph, even if it immediately explains that statement. Preserve the author's printed paragraphing rather than regrouping prose by semantic/argument cohesion. One quotation or one argument can contain several printed paragraphs. This is model input evidence, not a program ownership, deletion or acceptance rule. Conversely a mid-sentence line break, an OCR box boundary alone or sentence-final punctuation alone must not split a genuine paragraph. Decide each paragraph seam from its own reading continuity, not from the quotation as a whole. Set continues_from_previous=true only when continuing the same paragraph in the FINAL order within that ownership stream. On the first text in a stream it means append to the open output paragraph from preceding pages; if none exists, start with the available content. Do not copy initial flags without judging the actual text. Related subject matter is not enough: complete new sentences, quotations and independent explanations usually start new paragraphs. Omit continues_to_next; it is derived from successors. If you supply it, it must agree with the final successor. Images/tables may interrupt a paragraph; their own continuation flags are false. A standalone display formula breaks a paragraph. Independent publication cues and page numbers are ignored, with both flags false and all paragraph/citation fields null.

{% if includes_footnotes %}
Here citation means an explanatory footnote/endnote stream, NOT a generic group of numbered content. Exam questions and their subparts, numbered lists, exercises and section numbers remain paragraph content when they belong to the main document. A printed number alone does not establish a note or a reference. Keep such numbers in their original text; do not turn them into note marks or invent references to them. Genuine explanatory notes still use citation even when unnumbered or spanning several paragraphs.

Make the submitted fields express your reading decision, not merely the prose assessment. When correcting a mistaken note into main prose, change all affected members to ownership=paragraph, clear their citation_id/citation_index, remove the obsolete citation record and remove its references. Conversely, retain the definition, members and real references of a genuine note together. On reference-ownership feedback, reconsider whether the reference or the note itself was mistaken before moving the anchor; moving a reference between members of the same note cannot fix that conflict. Do not change correct reading ownership just to satisfy an anchor error.

Create one citation per logical note, not per OCR box. Multiple paragraphs, boxes and pages can belong to one note; several notes can share a box. Numbered definitions use page-local contiguous index=1,2,... and citation_id=new-PAGE-index. mark is the actual printed marker, or null if genuinely missing. Do not merge different printed notes or discard a definition to satisfy validation. All members of a note share its ID and citation_index. citations.stream_order defines note order. Every citation needs retained members.

Each citation_id is a target-page local handle, not a claim about a neighbour's identity or origin. Decide the citation OPERATION before assigning index or mark: is this an independent new note, or an append to a preceding note? For an append, write continues_previous=true, index=null and mark=null. The earlier note's number is background evidence only; never repeat it as if freshly printed on this page. A page-turn continuation cue is not a new printed note marker. A separately indented paragraph of that continued note still uses the append operation even when its own continues_from_previous=false. Only an independent new note receives a new local index. If your reading assessment says "continues the previous note", the citation record must express append, not a fresh definition. On field-conflict feedback repair the index/mark to express that same reading decision; do not flip continues_previous just to satisfy the schema. You may retain or replace initial IDs freely. Set citation.continues_previous=true only for the FIRST note that appends to the last open note output stream. This is an append operation, even when its first paragraph starts anew (continues_from_previous=false). Do not submit predecessor page/source ranges or copy a neighbour's membership as a requirement. Incoming notes have index=null and mark=null. If no output cursor exists (for example an excerpt starts here), their available content will be preserved as an explicitly unbound stream. A new unmarked independent note gets a new local index, with mark=null. Initial origins and grouping are guesses, not constraints.

References name the ORIGINAL layout_id, citation_index and anchor.before/mark/after taken consecutively from original layout.text. Make the anchor unique. They annotate content already owned by a paragraph; they do not consume another copy. A reference must fit within one retained body part. Associate only with the correct indexed target definition. Never swallow body text as a marker. A formula may be an OCR wrapper around a printed footnote superscript: for 2005^{9}, set extract_from_expression=true and select exactly ^{9} to retain the year 2005 and link note 9. Only an explicitly identified, complete terminal superscript with a real matching note can be extracted; do not reinterpret ordinary mathematical exponents as notes.

{% endif %}

Topic similarity does NOT distinguish a footnote from main prose: explanatory notes normally discuss the very same concepts as the body. When an unfinished main-text construction has a literal next-page completion, that is strong evidence for the main reading seam. Before calling an intervening unnumbered paragraph a return to body, mentally insert it between those two literal pieces and read the resulting main-text sentence. Do not interrupt a suspended list, quotation or parenthesis with an independent explanation merely because its topic resembles the body. Keep the explanatory note stream separate from that unfinished main construction.

Audit the reading streams before returning. First follow the main narrative through the raw neighbouring text, including any unfinished parenthesis, quotation or sentence at the page break. Then account for everything between that main-text ending and its next-page continuation. Numbered notes can contain several independent paragraphs and diagrams; their later paragraphs normally have NO repeated printed marker. Determine whether such a paragraph elaborates the last numbered note, instead of promoting it to body just because it opens a complete sentence, starts with a conjunction, or has no number. A note's paragraph break does not create a new note. Carry explanations and any intervening diagram in their logical note, while keeping genuine internal paragraph breaks. Diagram labels belong to the same note when that note introduces the diagram; use preserve_as_image for genuinely non-linear artwork. Page-turn cues are ignored publication apparatus, not note prose or sources that must be retained to permit continuation. Use neighbours only as raw evidence for your own current-page decisions; no neighbour's eventual decision will approve or veto them.
{% if not includes_footnotes %}
Footnote output is disabled, but identifying the full note stream is still necessary BEFORE excluding it. Internally follow each printed note through ALL of its unnumbered explanatory paragraphs and associated diagrams. Removing a numbered note does not make its next paragraph a body paragraph, and the ignored output itself is not evidence that the note has ended. Preserve genuine body that resumes after a note only when its words and the main narrative support that return. First-line indent is evidence about paragraphing, never evidence of body ownership. citations and references must be empty; citation_id and citation_index must be null on every entry. Retain genuine body even if OCR classified it as footnote. Exclude clearly identified note content, page numbers and publication cues as ignored. For a mixed source, use ranges to retain body and ignore the note; do not delete the whole box. If semantic evidence is insufficient, preserve the initial ownership. Do not infer exclusion just from bottom-of-page location, size or numbering.
{% endif %}

preserve_as_image=true is only for genuinely non-linear OCR diagrams whose labels cannot be read as prose. Preserve their ownership and original region; do not use it to avoid normal text or inline formulas. Set asset_attachment={"asset_id": "layout-ID", "slot": "title"|"caption"} to associate a retained text part with any retained image/table on THIS page, whether or not the initial algorithm suggested that link. A table's structured content source may keep slot="content"; an image's non-display content is never an editable display slot. Null asset_attachment detaches the text; there is no separate retention flag. You may create, remove, redirect or change a slot. Attached text and its asset must have the same submitted ownership and citation identity. The asset remains an asset: retain it unless it genuinely should be ignored; preserve_as_image is not needed for an existing image.

Sources named layout-ID/title, /caption or /content expose existing intrinsic DISPLAY fields. They are real source slots, not new OCR observations; they retain the original asset bbox, not a fabricated text rectangle. Account for them like every other source. Keep their initial attachment, move them to another slot/asset, or restore them to main prose/a note when the initial placement was wrong. Preserve structured table content; do not flatten or rewrite it. A source may be split into disjoint ranges to separate caption and body, and several sources may form one caption. The layouts array specifies final text order within each destination. Set paragraph connections within that destination; they must not connect a caption to main prose. Captions and titles begin and end on this page, with no cross-page connection flags.

For an independent description with no reliable asset target, set independent_stream to a nonempty page-local handle shared only by its own text parts, and leave asset_attachment null. Its ownership is paragraph, but its paragraphs are separate from main prose. Use its own internal order/paragraph breaks; it cannot append to preceding or following pages. Ordinary main text has independent_stream=null. Never invent a target, misclassify a caption as a footnote, or ignore real text merely to satisfy a paragraph seam. Never move or rewrite the asset itself. Follow all schema constraints and return a complete candidate, not a patch. On validation feedback reconsider the actual semantics; do not connect unrelated paragraphs or omit information just to pass.

printed_lines.left_offsets gives each observed ink band's left edge in pixels RELATIVE TO THIS SAME OCR RECTANGLE, in printed top-to-bottom order. In particular, a first offset farther right than the later offsets is a first-line-only inset; shifting an entire quotation or column shifts the rectangle too and cannot explain that difference. Read these actual local line starts when deciding the incoming paragraph boundary, separately from argument/quotation continuity or an outgoing page wrap. For a single-line rectangle, its bbox left is its actual first-line start; for a multi-line rectangle, the bbox left alone is not. These are physical observations only, not program paragraph verdicts. Use literal incoming sentence continuity as well as the printed boundary; do not merge independent indented explanations just because they explain the preceding assertion.
